> Jove <invalid@invalid.invalid> writes:
> >   It may not be possible to polymorph an Arch-Lich.  Monster Magic
> > Resistance isn't used in a straightforward manner.  Quoting Dylan
> > O'Donnell:
> >
> > :>>In Nethack, monsters' magic resistance saving throws
> > :>>are a modified-percentile roll, with the modifiers being applied to
> > :>>the _denominator_ rather than the numerator;
> > :>>
> > :>>  (base MR)/(100 + attacker level - defender level)
> > :>>
> > :>> where "attacker level" is your XL for spells, and fixed levels for
> > :>>object effects:
> > :>>
> > :>>  Player XL for spells
> > :>>         12 for wands,
> > :>>         10 for tools and artifact weapons,
> > :>>          9 for scrolls,
> > :>>          6 for potions, and
> > :>>          5 for rings.
> >
> >   An Arch-Lich is Level 25, so the result is:  90/(75 + attacker level).
>
> An arch-lich's _base_ level is 25; this gets adjusted at creation for
> player level and depth, and may be affected by draining, PoGL, all the
> usual stuff. (But the initial adjustment isn't going to take it below
> 24, so it's not all that significant here.)
>
> > I don't know what the attacker level is for a polymorph trap.  Unless
> > it's at least 16 the Arch-Lich is immune to it.
>
> Polytraps are resisted as if they were wand effects, so 12.
